##
   `mut var` permits reassigning the binding, not writing through its type. A
   field write goes through the type, so this is rejected -- and reported as a
   write through an immutable type, which is the distinction that tells the user
   what to change (`mut var s: mut S`, not a different binding-level keyword).
##
struct Point { x: i32 }

func bad(): i32 {
  mut var s = .Point{.x = 1i32};
  s.x = 5i32;
  return s.x;
}
